every morning Sunil Kumar shows up for a  job that few anywhere would want to do  as a manual scavenger he cleans block  drains and gutters in Dennis Sunil has  been doing this for six years he earns  anywhere between 400 to 800 rupees a day  that's about five to ten euros you  almost threw up the first day here until  then I've never cleaned the toilet in my  own home the stench was disgusting  I nearly quit but I had to feed my two  children since I'd lost another job so I  carried on the work is extremely  dangerous there's no machinery or safety  equipment sometimes so needless to  undress and squeeze into the sewers to  unplug them  he says he's developed skin allergies  his eyes burn from the toxic gases the  labourers belonged to the balm icky  community that sits at the bottom of  India's caste system their work opens  them to prejudice and discrimination  some people treat us as if we're not  human  we're human too now you're sitting here  it's very dirty but people cover their  noses when they walk past why is that  manual scavenging was outlawed in India  more than two decades ago the law  prescribes fines and even jail time for  those employing workers to manually  clean toilets sewage systems and septic  tanks without protective gear but the  rules are routinely flouted with  devastating consequences  this settlement on the outskirts of  Delhi is home to Dalits  formerly known as Untouchables two  months ago three of its residents choked  to death in a septic tank in a car parts  factory  all three were cleaners none had safety  equipment one of them was nanny his wife  Poonam is still waiting for compensation  for the company my children don't  understand that their father is not  coming back  our world has come crashing down I've  had to borrow a lot of money since I  lost my husband and I don't know how I'm  going to pay it back  the issue has sparked an outcry reports  in the Indian media say more than 10  sewage workers have died in Delhi alone  in recent months the city government has  since banned its agencies from  outsourcing sanitation work to  contractors who hire manual scavengers  Ashish Keeton  a spokesman for the Delhi government  says manual scavenging has no place in  modern India but he admits the rot runs  much deeper  it's absolutely inhuman its medieval  there is a problem of lack of awareness  there is problem of insensitivity and  just callous attitude on the part of not  just the police but I am afraid large  sections of our society because they  don't see it as a as wrong as as a moral  wrong as a legal wrong that means little  - Sunil and his friends they finished  here  they're already off to the next job  regardless of the law they will always  be enough demand for cheap labor to do  the city's dirty work
 

